   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.1",
    "topic_title": "Enzymes",
    "stem": "An enzyme’s active site has a shape and charge distribution that fit substrate X but not substrate Y. Under otherwise identical conditions, how should the enzyme affect the reaction rates of these substrates, and why?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "It increases the reaction rate for X, but not Y, because the enzyme supplies X with the energy needed to react.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Energy-donor misconception: this treats the enzyme as an energy source. Enzymes accelerate reactions by lowering the activation-energy barrier, not by supplying substrates with energy. Fix: Identify whether the explanation lowers an energy barrier or supplies energy."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "It increases the reaction rate for X, but not Y, because binding X allows the enzyme to lower the reaction’s activation energy.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "X fits the active site and can form an enzyme–substrate complex. The enzyme lowers the activation energy, allowing X to react faster; Y cannot bind and therefore receives no catalytic benefit."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "It increases the reaction rate for X, but not Y, because binding X raises the reaction’s activation energy.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Activation-energy reversal: this confuses a higher energy barrier with faster reaction. Raising activation energy would make the reaction slower under the same conditions; enzyme catalysis lowers that barrier. Fix: Check that a faster reaction is linked to a lower activation-energy barrier."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "It increases the reaction rates for both X and Y because enzymes lower activation energy regardless of active-site fit.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Specificity misconception: this assumes an enzyme catalyzes reactions for substrates that cannot bind. Active-site shape and charge determine substrate recognition, so Y cannot receive catalytic assistance from this enzyme. Fix: Check each substrate’s shape and charge against the active site before predicting catalysis."
     }
    ]
   },
